For each fixed $S$ there is a finite $\beta_0(S)$ such that, for every fixed finite $\beta\ge\beta_0(S)$, one has $m=m_{S,\beta}>0$ and $$\label{eq:spherical-law}
 \lim_{\substack{L\to\infty\\L\text{ even}}}
  \left\langle
     \exp\!\left(\frac1V\sum_{a=1}^3t_aM_L^a\right)
  \right\rangle_{\beta,L}
   =\frac1{4\pi}\int_{\mathbb S^2}e^{m t\cdot n}\,\mathrm d\sigma(n)
   =\frac{\sinh(m|t|)}{m|t|}
 \qquad(t\in\mathbb R^3).$$ Here $\sigma$ is surface-area measure on $\mathbb S^2$, and the quotient at $t=0$ is interpreted as $1$. In addition, $$\label{eq:second-moment-law}
 \lim_{\substack{L\to\infty\\L\text{ even}}}
   \frac1{V^2}\sum_{x,y\in\Lambda_L}
          \langle S_x\cdot S_y\rangle_{\beta,L}=m^2.$$

For the finite picture above the following assertions hold.*

1.  *For every finite $E$ and nonempty single-time $T$ disjoint from $E$, $$\label{eq:simultaneous-pin}
                      \mathbb P_E(T\subseteq\mathcal B_E)
                                      \le(2r(E,T))^{|T|}.$$*

2.  *Almost surely under $\mathbb P_P$, $g_P(\mathbf a):=\mathbb Q_{\mathbf a}(G_P)>0$, and the conditional law of the remaining picture is $\mathbb Q_{\mathbf a}(\,\cdot\mid G_P)$.*


3.  *Let $\omega_{\rm m}$ be the Poisson mark counting measure and let $\lambda_{\rm b}$ be edge rate times Lebesgue time measure. For every integer $j\ge1$ and nonnegative measurable function $F$ of $j$ mark locations, $$\label{eq:factorial-domination}
     \mathbb E_E\sum_{(u_1,\ldots,u_j)\in\omega_{\rm m,\ne}^j}
                    F(u_1,\ldots,u_j)
           \le 2^j\int F\,d\lambda_{\rm b}^{\otimes j}.$$ The sum is over ordered distinct marks, and may be restricted to any prescribed time order. Independent additional fresh Poisson clocks may be included with their own, undoubled, factorial intensity.

Let $\Pi$ have an admissible permutation law on a finite set $\mathcal V=I\sqcup D$, and put $K=\mathbb E\Pi$. Let $G_I$ be the event that every permutation cycle meets $I$. On $G_I$, let $\Sigma$ be the permutation matrix on $I$ taking each pin to its next positive visit to $I$. Then $$\label{eq:all-hit}
                    \Pr(G_I)=\det(I_D-K_{DD}).$$ For every complex matrix $W$ on $I$, there is the polynomial identity $$\label{eq:next-pin-determinant}
 \mathbb E[\mathbf 1_{G_I}\det(I_I-W\Sigma)]
  =\det\begin{pmatrix}
       I_I-WK_{II}&-WK_{ID}\\
       -K_{DI}&I_D-K_{DD}
       \end{pmatrix}.$$ If $\Pr(G_I)>0$ and $I\ne\varnothing$, then $K_{DD}$ is transient and $$\label{eq:next-pin-mean}
 \mathbb E[\Sigma\mid G_I]
    =K_{II}+K_{ID}(I_D-K_{DD})^{-1}K_{DI}.$$ This is the first positive hitting kernel of $I$ for the Markov chain with independent transitions $K$. Empty determinants are one and empty correction terms are zero. In particular <a href="#eq:next-pin-determinant" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:next-pin-determinant">[eq:next-pin-determinant]</a>, and its linear coefficients in $W$, remain valid when the all-hit probability is zero.

Fix $S$ and $C_s$. There is $\beta_0<\infty$ such that, for every fixed $\beta\ge\beta_0$, there is $L_*(\beta)<\infty$ with the following property. For every even $L\ge L_*(\beta)$, every $q$ satisfying $L^{-5/2}\le q<1$, every pin set $E$ as above, and every sparse set $T$ of real slot points at one deterministic time, $$\label{eq:sparse-pins}
 \mathbb P_E(T\subseteq\mathcal B_E)\le p^{|T|},
 \qquad p=\beta^{-9/10}.$$ The constants and thresholds are independent of the number, locations, and times of the extra pins.

In an available-slot graph regular at $z$ with radius $A\ge A_0$, put $$\mathcal D(v)=\tfrac12\sum_{\{i,j\}\text{ available edge}}
                              (v_i-v_j)^2.$$ Every nonnegative $v$ supported over $B_A(z)$ satisfies $$\label{eq:local-nash}
 \lVert v\rVert_2^{10/3}\le C\mathcal D(v)\lVert v\rVert_1^{4/3}.$$ For every available slot $a$ at $y\in B_A(z)$, there exists an oriented edge flow $F_a$, with divergence defined as outgoing minus incoming flow, such that $$\label{eq:unit-flow}
 \mathop{\mathrm{div}}F_a=\mathbf 1_{\{a\}},\qquad
 |F_a(e)|\le C(1+\mathop{\mathrm{dist}}(e,y))^{-k},\qquad
 k=2-3\nu=199/100.$$ Here $\mathop{\mathrm{dist}}(e,y)$ is the minimum supremum distance to the two endpoint sites. The constants are uniform over the graph, $A$, $z$, and the source. Sites with any number of available slots between one and $\ell$ are permitted.

Under $\mathsf I(P)$, at any deterministic time $t$, $$\begin{aligned}
 \mathbb P_P(A'\subseteq\mathcal O_t)&\le Cp^6
 &&\text{for six prescribed distinct sites},\label{eq:six-blocking}\\
 \mathbb P_P(A'\subseteq\mathcal O_t)&\le(C\sqrt p)^{|A'|}
 &&\text{for a nonempty sparse set of sites}.\label{eq:sparse-blocking}
\end{aligned}$$ There are $c,C,\lambda>0$, uniform for sufficiently large $\beta$, such that the blocked component formed with steps of supremum length at most eight satisfies $$\label{eq:torus-component-tail}
 \mathbb P_P\{z\in\mathcal O_t,\ 
       \max_{v\in\mathcal C_t(z)}d_L(z,v)\ge h\}
       \le C e^{-ch^\lambda}\qquad(h\ge1).$$ All constants are independent of the grid and its size.

Assume $\mathsf I(P)$ and <a href="#eq:sparse-localization-radius" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:sparse-localization-radius">[eq:sparse-localization-radius]</a>. For every deterministic time and anchor, failure of regularity has probability at most $$\label{eq:torus-irregularity}
 \Delta_A=CA^3p^6+C\exp(-cA^{\lambda\nu}).$$ Call an exposure good at the anchor if its nonregular times have total length at most $\beta/100$. Its probability of not being good is at most $100\Delta_A$. On a good exposure, the one-turn kernel localized by killing outside $B_A(z)$ has every entry at most $C\beta^{-3/2}$, in either direction.*

*If a deterministic starting slot is a hole almost surely, localization at its site loses at most $Ce^{-c\beta}$ expected mass in any forward or reversed traversal piece of length at most one turn. This last assertion does not require $\mathsf I(P)$.

At a fixed slice let $(z_i)_{i=1}^{m_T}$ be a sparse list, including repetitions. Retain any subset of its members at which the lifted graph is regular with radius $A$. For each retained member let $\mu_i\ge0$ be a mass at most one, supported on the real holes over $B_A(z_i)$. If $L$ satisfies <a href="#eq:sparse-localization-radius" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:sparse-localization-radius">[eq:sparse-localization-radius]</a> and is sufficiently large compared with $A$, then every real vector $Y'$ on the holes of that slice satisfies $$\label{eq:torus-flow-pairing}
 \left|\left\langle\sum_i\mu_i,Y'\right\rangle\right|
 \le (Cm_TA^\alpha)^{1/2}\sqrt{\mathcal D_t(Y')}
      +Cm_TL^{1/2-k}\lVert Y'\rVert_2.$$

Fix $S$. For all sufficiently large $\beta$ there are $C_\beta,R_0(\beta)<\infty$ with the following property. Let $L$ be even, let $R_0(\beta)\le R\le L$, and partition $\Lambda_L$ into rectangular cells whose side lengths belong to $[R/2,4R]$. For every deterministic real $f:\Lambda_L\to[-1,1]$ with $\sum_{x\in B}f_x=0$ on each cell $B$, put $$D_f=\sum_x f_x Z_x,\qquad q=R^{-5/2},\qquad
 w=1-q=e^{-b}.$$ If $\langle\cdot\rangle_b$ is the Gibbs expectation with exponent $H_0+b\sum_xY_x$, then $$\label{eq:weak-field}
 \frac qV\langle D_f^2\rangle_b\le C_\beta R^{-1/40}.$$ The constants are uniform in the partition, $f$, and $L$.

Given the exposure $\mathbf a$, for $i,j\in\mathcal H$, $i\ne j$, $$\begin{aligned}
 \mathbb P(i\text{ is pinned}\mid\mathbf a)
   &=q(D^{-1})_{ii},\label{eq:pin-marginal}\\
 \mathbb P(i,j\text{ are pinned, next pin from }i\text{ is }j
            \mid\mathbf a)
   &=\frac{q^2}{w}(D^{-1})_{ij}.\label{eq:pin-successor}
\end{aligned}$$ Moreover, $$\label{eq:resolvent-trace}
 \mathbb E\mathop{\mathrm{Tr}}D^{-1}\le N.$$ For $f_i=f_{x_i}$ on the slots, $$\label{eq:transverse-resolvent}
 \langle D_f^2\rangle_b
 =\frac14\sum_i f_i^2+
   \frac12\mathbb E\sum_{\substack{i,j\in\mathcal H\\i\ne j}}
                   f_if_j(D^{-1})_{ij}.$$ All expectations here include the normalized picture and soft-pin law.

For every cell $B$, $$\label{eq:hole-cancellation}
 \mathbb E\left|\sum_{\substack{i\in\mathcal H\\x_i\in B}}f_i\right|
 \le C\sqrt{|B|/q}.$$

An endpoint edge from $i$ to $j$ satisfies $(u_i-u_j)^2\le C_\beta e_i$. For a contact edge witnessed by a fixed-position interval of length $\tau\le1$, $$\label{eq:contact-cost}
 (u_i-u_j)^2\le C_\beta\tau^{-1}(e_i+e_j).$$ The kernel $K$ is closed on every connected component of $\Gamma$, and $$\label{eq:component-count}
 \mathbb E\,\#\{\text{components of }\Gamma\}\le qN.$$

There are $\beta_0,c,C,\lambda_1>0$ and, for each $\beta\ge\beta_0$, a number $\delta_0(\beta)>0$ such that for every $0<\delta\le\delta_0(\beta)$, uniformly in the admissible volumes and pin parameters of this section, $$\label{eq:bad-box-tail}
 \mathbb P\{z\text{ is bad and its bad component reaches distance }h
          \text{ from }z\}
 \le C e^{-ch^{\lambda_1}}.$$ A specified box has a short-time flag with probability at most $C_\beta\delta$.

There are random numbers $c_B\in[-1,1]$, one per cell, such that $$\label{eq:cell-resolvent}
 \mathbb E\sum_B\sum_{\substack{i\in\mathcal H\\x_i\in B}}
        (u_i-c_B)^2\le C_\beta N R^{-1/20}.$$

Fix $S$ and a sufficiently large finite $\beta$. There are $R_0=R_0(S,\beta)$, $C_\beta<\infty$, and $\kappa>0$ with the following property. Let $L\ge R\ge R_0$, with $L$ even, and partition $\Lambda_L$ into rectangular cells whose side lengths lie in $[R/2,4R]$. If $f:\Lambda_L\to[-1,1]$ has sum zero on every cell, then, for $q=R^{-5/2}$, $b=-\log(1-q)$, and any spin component $a$, $$\label{eq:zero-exp}
 \log\left\langle
   \exp\left(bx\sum_{z\in\Lambda_L}f_zS_z^a\right)
 \right\rangle
 \le C_\beta bV,
 \qquad x\in\mathbb R,\quad |x|\le\kappa\log R.$$ Consequently there are $A_\beta,c>0$ such that the spectral measurement of $D_f=\sum_z f_zS_z^a$ in the zero-field state satisfies $$\label{eq:zero-tail}
 \mathbb P\left\{|D_f|>\frac{A_\beta V}{\log R}\right\}
       \le 2e^{-cbV}.$$ All bounds are uniform over the indicated partitions and tests.

The mean entropy exists on $\mathcal S_{\rm TI}$, is upper semicontinuous and affine, and $$\label{eq:variational-pressure}
 P_a(t)=\max_{\omega\in\mathcal S_{\rm TI}}
       \{\mathfrak s(\omega)+e_0(\omega)+t\omega(a)\}.$$ In particular, writing $P_0=\beta p_{S,\beta}(0)$, the set $$\mathcal E_\beta=
 \{\omega\in\mathcal S_{\rm TI}:
            \mathfrak s(\omega)+e_0(\omega)=P_0\}$$ is a nonempty compact face of $\mathcal S_{\rm TI}$. Every local limit of periodic zero-field Gibbs states belongs to $\mathcal E_\beta$, and $$\label{eq:pressure-tangent}
 P_a'(0+)=\max_{\omega\in\mathcal E_\beta}\omega(a).$$ The extreme points of $\mathcal E_\beta$ are precisely its states that are extreme in $\mathcal S_{\rm TI}$; we call these states ergodic.

For every finite nonempty rectangle $C\subset\mathbb Z^3$ and every translation-invariant equilibrium state $\omega$, $$\label{eq:local-second-moment}
 \sum_{a=1}^3\omega\!\left[
       \left(\frac1{|C|}\sum_{x\in C}S_x^a\right)^2\right]\ge m^2.$$

The periodic zero-field Gibbs states satisfy $$\label{eq:global-second-lower}
 \liminf_{L\to\infty}\frac1{V^2}
        \sum_{a=1}^3\langle(M_L^a)^2\rangle\ge m^2.$$
